Ohle Claussen <claussen@dpi.physik.uni-goettingen.de> writes:

> I can only think of two reasons for a pet not to help you; first, when it's
> busy eating. The second reason is that the opponents are of a higher 
> level than your pet, or your pet is badly hurt. 

Just as a nitpicking side-remark:  Pets might also be paralyzed or 
sleeping, although this is admittedly rare and can usually be 
easily noticed.  

> If you have a stethoscope, you can
> investigate this. Try to guess wether your pet is injured, and heal it
> appropriately (spell of healing, wielded potion). 

Well...  spending valuable potions of healing for a non-endangered pet 
(unless it attacks, it is mostly safe) does not meet my personal tactical 
preference list.  Might be efficient in some situations, but I'd not do 
that regularly.  

Much more important strategy is to run around your pet while it's 
busy eating or healing up; running away cleverly is in general a 
great idea to prolong survival in the dungeons.  

> Also make sure that your pet levels up sufficiently. Pets also do this by 
> killing monsters, but for them, every kill is worth the same. 

This is true in [s], but not in nh.  In nh, the pet gets advancement 
proportional to monster hit points.  You don't really see the difference 
early on, though.  

